---
import PNotes from "../components/PNotes.astro";
---

<p-slide class="cover-internal-sub-section">
  <h2>HTTP</h2>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">Project setup</h3>
  <pre class="small-line" data-label-file="package.json">
<code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">
    {
      "name": "http",
      "type": "module",
      "scripts": {
        "start": "node server.js"
      },
      "dependencies": {
        "@modelcontextprotocol/server": "^2.0.0",
        "@modelcontextprotocol/express": "^2.0.0",
        "@modelcontextprotocol/node": "^2.0.0",
        "express": "^5.0.0",
        "zod": "^4.0.0"
      }
    }</code>
  </pre>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Same as stdio, plus Express and two small adapters: the Express
      integration and the Node.js request handler bridge. Streamable HTTP
      support lives in the server package itself.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide follow-fragments>
  <h3 class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">Creating the server</h3>
  <pre class="small-line" data-label-file="server.js">
<code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">
    import { createMcpHandler } from "@modelcontextprotocol/server";
    import { createMcpExpressApp } from "@modelcontextprotocol/express";
    import { toNodeHandler } from "@modelcontextprotocol/node";
    // createServer() — same factory as stdio
    </code><p-fragment><code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">
    const handler = </code><code p-fragment p-effect="highlight" style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">createMcpHandler(createServer);</code>
<code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">    const node = toNodeHandler(handler);

    const app = createMcpExpressApp();
    app.all("/mcp", (req, res) => node(req, res, req.body));

    app.listen(3000);</code></p-fragment></pre>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      The tool registration is identical to stdio — we reuse the exact same
      createServer factory. Tools are transport-agnostic.
    </p>
    <p>
      createMcpHandler turns the factory into a Streamable HTTP handler. Since
      the 2026-07-28 spec MCP is stateless: no initialize handshake, no session
      ID, every request carries its own protocol version. That means any
      instance behind a plain round-robin load balancer can answer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Older 2025-era clients are still served by default, statelessly, from the
      same factory — so upgrading the server doesn't break existing clients.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">Connecting a client</h3>
  <pre class="small-line" data-label-file="mcp.json">
<code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">
    {
      "mcpServers": {
        "my-http-mcp-server": {
          "type": "http",
          "url": "http://localhost:3000/mcp"
        }
      }
    }</code>
  </pre>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Instead of a command and a path, the client just needs a URL. The server
      can run anywhere — locally, on a VPS, or on a cloud platform.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
